<!DOCTYPE article PUBLIC "-//NLM//DTD Journal Archiving and Interchange DTD v2.3 20070202//EN" "archivearticle.dtd"><article xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" article-type="research-article"><?properties open_access?><front><journal-meta><journal-id journal-id-type="nlm-ta">Dev Immunol</journal-id><journal-title>Developmental Immunology</journal-title><issn pub-type="ppub">1044-6672</issn><publisher><publisher-name>Hindawi Publishing Corporation</publisher-name></publisher></journal-meta><article-meta><article-id pub-id-type="pmid">11785668</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="pmc">2276073</article-id>        <article-id pub-id-type="pii">S1740252201752596</article-id>        <article-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1155/2001/75259</article-id>    <article-categories><subj-group subj-group-type="heading"><subject>Research Article</subject></subj-group></article-categories><title-group><article-title>Incidence of Apoptosis in the Lymphoid Organs of Normalor Malaria Infected Mice is Decreased in CD18 andUrokinase - Receptor (UPAR, CD87) Deficient Mice</article-title></title-group><contrib-group>        <contrib contrib-type="author" corresp="yes"><name><surname>Piguet</surname><given-names>Pierre Francois</given-names></name></contrib>        <contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>da Laperrousaz</surname><given-names>Chen</given-names></name></contrib>                    <contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Vesin</surname><given-names>Christian</given-names></name></contrib>                    <contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Donati</surname><given-names>Yves</given-names></name></contrib>                    </contrib-group>            <aff id="aff"><label/>                <addr-line>Department of Pathology</addr-line>                <addr-line>University of Geneva</addr-line>                                <addr-line>1 rue M. Servet</addr-line>                        <addr-line>CMU</addr-line>                        <addr-line>CH 1211</addr-line>                <country>Switzerland</country>            </aff>        <pub-date pub-type="ppub"><year>2001</year></pub-date><volume>8</volume><issue>3-4</issue><fpage>183</fpage><lpage>191</lpage><permissions><copyright-statement>Copyright &#x000a9; 2001 Hindawi Publishing Corporation.</copyright-statement><copyright-year>2001</copyright-year><copyright-holder>Hindawi Publishing Corporation</copyright-holder><license license-type="open-access" xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/"><p>This is an open access article distributed under the Creative Commons Attribution License, which permits unrestricted use, distribution, and reproduction in any medium, provided the original work is properly cited.</p></license></permissions><abstract>            <p>Incidence of apoptosis was investigated in the spleen and lymph nodes of +/+, CD18 -/- andurokinase receptor (uPAR, CD87) -/- mice, untreated or <italic>Plasmodium Berghei Anka</italic> (PbA)infected. In non infected mice, incidence of apoptosis was lower in the lymph nodes of CD18-/- and uPAR -/- than in +/+ mice, as seen by FACS analysis to count the number of hypodiploidand Annexin-V binding cells. Infection of mice with PbA resulted in a marked increasein the size of spleen and lymph nodes 7&#x02013;8 days after infection, which was slightly higher inuPAR -/- and CD18 -/- than in +/+ mice. PbA infection increased about 7 fold the incidence ofapoptosis in the lymphoid organs of +/+, especially in the white pulp and germinal centers ofthe spleen and lymph nodes, while in contrast it was unchanged in PbA infected CD18 -/- oruPAR -/- mice. Serum IgG levels, and number of circulating leukocytes were significantlyhigher in both uPAR and CD18 -/- than in +/+ mice. These results indicate that the CD18 anduPAR surface molecules, which are known to be associated in the cell membrane, have animportant influence upon the incidence of cell survival in both normal or stimulated lymphoidorgans.</p>        </abstract><kwd-group><kwd>malaria</kwd><kwd>CD18</kwd><kwd>CD87</kwd><kwd>apoptosis</kwd><kwd>IgG</kwd><kwd>lymphocyte</kwd></kwd-group></article-meta></front></article>